measurementTechnique:
Tree Harvests:
Individual trees with circumference at 1.3 m (DBH) ranging from 0.05 to 0.4 m and height from less than <1 to 8.4 m were cut at the surface of the S1 Bog, and brought to a central location for dimensional measurements including:
Total tree height (m) to the nearest 0.1 m;
Basal circumference in cm;
DBH circumference (cm at 1.3 meters above the base); and
Maximum canopy width (m).
Once these measurements were obtained, the tree was cut into 1 meter segments and wet mass assessments were done for the bole and combined branch/needle materials. A bole sample or ‘cookie’ from the base of each segment was saved for wet/dry mass assessments along with a representative branch. Other measures collected for each 1-m section of each tree include:
Circumference at the base (cm);
Number of branches;
Number of years of needles held on a typical branch (determined only for some trees);
Sapwood thickness (radial thickness in mm);
Bole wet mass (g);
Branch/foliage wet mass (g) for all branches combined; and
Branch/foliage wet mass (g) for the representative branch.
Wet tissue samples were frozen and then shipped to Oak Ridge, Tennessee. Dry mass of samples was determined after drying overnight at 65 degrees C. Water content correction factors were obtained to convert wet mass tissue data to total dry mass assessments (g) for each measured tree.
